{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>Fools mock at guilt</b> — each one of them, and it is from the language of speech, like 'the one who interpreted between them,' meaning he advocates to make a guilt-offering." "<b>but among the upright there is favor</b> — that a man desires his fellow. And its meaning is like: 'and your sin — like Sodom, declare it'. Another interpretation: when he brings a guilt-offering he mocks and says it is of no avail; therefore they do not bring it, for a boor does not fear sin, and so with all the statutes. But the guilt-offering among the upright is favor before HaMakom, for through it their iniquities are atoned. Or: they mock at sin, and therefore they are not concerned to rob or extort or cause wrongdoing, but among the upright you find only the favor of HaMakom and of men, which is the straight path; and according to this interpretation he said 'favor' without specification."], :book :proverbs, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {1 "Resolved המקום as HaMakom throughout; Ibn Ezra's standard reference to God. The first quoted phrase in [1] alludes to Isaiah 1:18 (ואם חטאתם כשני תאדמו), compressed as typical; translated the allusion as he cites it."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Proverbs -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/mishley/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:22:44.599174087Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}